WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:05.213
In this course, you've learned
about the core concepts of MCP.

2
00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:09.384
You've built a server that exposes tools,
resources, and prompts,

3
00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:12.971
developed a chatbot,
that can connect to multiple servers,

4
00:00:13.054 --> 00:00:15.724
use Claude Desktop
to build more sophisticated

5
00:00:15.724 --> 00:00:18.685
applications
and deploy your own remote server.

6
00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:20.145
Congratulations!

7
00:00:20.145 --> 00:00:22.355
MCP is constantly evolving.

8
00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:25.608
In this last lesson,
you'll learn about other features of MCP

9
00:00:25.942 --> 00:00:28.945
and some of the exciting things
coming soon to the protocol.

10
00:00:29.446 --> 00:00:30.989
I'll see you there.

11
00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:33.867
There's a lot you've learned
about the Model Context Protocol.

12
00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:38.121
You've learned about hosts and clients
and servers, tools, resources and prompts.

13
00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:39.748
And then you've got a chance

14
00:00:39.748 --> 00:00:44.002
to write some code to power larger
applications using all of these ideas.

15
00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:46.129
But there's still a bit
that we have not yet covered

16
00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:47.756
about the model context protocol.

17
00:00:47.756 --> 00:00:51.301
Much of this is an active development,
and you can always examine the latest

18
00:00:51.593 --> 00:00:53.845
on the specification, run through GitHub

19
00:00:53.845 --> 00:00:55.889
and through the discussions
that you can find.

20
00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:56.514
The first piece

21
00:00:56.514 --> 00:01:00.060
we haven't covered yet is authentication
in the Model Context Protocol.

22
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:04.147
In the March specification update,
OAUTH 2.1 was added

23
00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:07.317
as the means of authentication
with remote servers.

24
00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:10.361
What this allows for
is for clients and servers

25
00:01:10.361 --> 00:01:13.782
to authenticate and send authenticated
requests to data sources.

26
00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.993
You can imagine many different servers
need to access data

27
00:01:17.118 --> 00:01:19.204
that requires some form of authentication.

28
00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:22.707
This requires the client
making a request to the server.

29
00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:26.419
The server
then requiring some user to authenticate.

30
00:01:26.544 --> 00:01:29.547
And once the authentication process
is successfully done,

31
00:01:29.714 --> 00:01:34.344
the client and server can exchange a token
and the client can make authenticated

32
00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:37.347
requests to the server
and then to the data source.

33
00:01:37.472 --> 00:01:39.849
This part of the protocol
is an active development,

34
00:01:39.849 --> 00:01:43.144
and there are always newer features
and security pieces being added.

35
00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:46.439
But authentication will primarily be done
with the OAUTH 2.1

36
00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:49.442
protocol. To highlight
that in some depth,

37
00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:52.278
this is an optional feature
of the Model Context Protocol,

38
00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:55.782
but it is highly recommended
for remote servers. With standard IO,

39
00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.743
we use environment variables
and don't have the need

40
00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:00.245
for this kind of authentication.

41
00:02:00.245 --> 00:02:03.289
This is built on established standards
that you can take a look at with

42
00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:04.499
these links below.

43
00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:08.378
So while we've explored primitives
that to be exposed by the server

44
00:02:09.087 --> 00:02:13.800
tools, resources and prompts, we also have
primitives that the clients can expose.

45
00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:16.469
These include roots and sampling.

46
00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:17.637
Let's dive into this.

47
00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:22.142
A route is a URI that a client suggests
a server should operate in.

48
00:02:22.392 --> 00:02:26.187
The idea is centered around
only looking in specific folders

49
00:02:26.187 --> 00:02:29.983
for files that you might need.
When the client connects to a server,

50
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:33.153
it can declare the roots
that the server should work with.

51
00:02:33.194 --> 00:02:35.446
This can be useful for filesystem paths,

52
00:02:35.446 --> 00:02:39.200
but can be any valid URI,
including HTTP URLs.

53
00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:42.745
The benefits of roots
allow for security limitations.

54
00:02:42.745 --> 00:02:46.666
Allow for keeping the server focused
on a relevant file path or location,

55
00:02:47.041 --> 00:02:50.086
and roots also have some versatility
baked in, where again,

56
00:02:50.086 --> 00:02:54.007
they're useful for file paths,
but also can be any valid HTTP URI.

57
00:02:54.382 --> 00:02:57.510
We're slowly starting to see more
and more clients adapt this primitive,

58
00:02:57.719 --> 00:03:00.638
and it's an important one
to keep track of as the protocol evolves.

59
00:03:00.638 --> 00:03:03.641
The last primitive
that we're going to cover is sampling.

60
00:03:03.808 --> 00:03:08.605
Sampling allows for servers to request
inference from a large language model.

61
00:03:09.063 --> 00:03:11.441
Kind of like the other side
of communication,

62
00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:14.277
where instead of the client
talking to the large language model,

63
00:03:14.277 --> 00:03:17.405
the server can talk back to the client
and request inference.

64
00:03:17.822 --> 00:03:19.824
An example here might be a situation

65
00:03:19.824 --> 00:03:22.952
where your users report
that a website is slow for some reason.

66
00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:28.411
Your MCP server can then collect server
logs, performance metrics, error logs,

67
00:03:28.583 --> 00:03:32.003
and communicate with a variety of data
sources to see what's going on.

68
00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:35.131
Instead of the server
handing that back to the client,

69
00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:37.467
putting everything in the context window,

70
00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:40.178
or potentially
any kind of breach of security

71
00:03:40.178 --> 00:03:43.890
between the server and the client,
the server instead can talk directly

72
00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.435
to the large language model
and ask it to diagnose performance issues.

73
00:03:47.685 --> 00:03:51.481
The large language model analyzes
the patterns and returns data back,

74
00:03:51.814 --> 00:03:55.610
and then the server can generate the steps
to make the website a bit less slow.

75
00:03:55.902 --> 00:04:00.114
When there are concerns from a security
standpoint or breaching boundaries.

76
00:04:00.114 --> 00:04:04.035
Or you might not want all that data
coming back to be put into context.

77
00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.871
Sampling and creating sampling loops,

78
00:04:06.871 --> 00:04:11.626
it's a very powerful way for servers
to request inference and kind of switch

79
00:04:11.626 --> 00:04:14.629
the direction of communication
from what we've seen before.

80
00:04:14.837 --> 00:04:16.214
This is also quite powerful

81
00:04:16.214 --> 00:04:19.467
as we explore agentic capabilities
with the Model Context Protocol.

82
00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:20.843
As we start to move towards

83
00:04:20.843 --> 00:04:24.138
a world where more models
are talking to different data sources,

84
00:04:24.722 --> 00:04:28.017
and we're giving more autonomy
to models to call different tools

85
00:04:28.017 --> 00:04:29.310
and go off on their own,

86
00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.480
we believe MCP
will be a foundational protocol for agents.

87
00:04:33.064 --> 00:04:37.527
As we start to think about how MCP
can be used with agentic capabilities,

88
00:04:38.027 --> 00:04:41.656
you can imagine a scenario
where an user and a large language model

89
00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:45.118
need to access a variety of MCP servers.

90
00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.162
What's so powerful about the Model
Context Protocol is

91
00:04:48.162 --> 00:04:51.165
that there's a composable
and somewhat recursive nature,

92
00:04:51.207 --> 00:04:54.210
where clients can be servers,
and servers can be clients.

93
00:04:54.502 --> 00:04:58.172
What this allows us to do
is to start creating an architecture

94
00:04:58.423 --> 00:05:02.135
where we can take advantage of the ability
for clients to communicate with servers,

95
00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:03.886
but also for servers

96
00:05:03.886 --> 00:05:07.890
to request the data that they need
through sampling back to a client.

97
00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:11.394
What we've set up here
is the idea of a multi-agent architecture,

98
00:05:11.853 --> 00:05:16.024
where the application and a large language
model communicate with an agent.

99
00:05:16.566 --> 00:05:20.111
This agent happens to be an MCP
client and a server,

100
00:05:20.611 --> 00:05:23.114
and it can serve data
back to the application,

101
00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:25.992
but it can also connect to other clients

102
00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:28.995
and servers
through the Model Context Protocol.

103
00:05:29.037 --> 00:05:32.248
You can imagine that we have agents
for analysis, for coding,

104
00:05:32.248 --> 00:05:35.668
for research
that also happen to be MCP servers.

105
00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:39.130
And if they need to connect
to other servers, clients as well.

106
00:05:39.422 --> 00:05:40.798
Through this composable nature,

107
00:05:40.798 --> 00:05:44.635
we can start to think about architectures
that allow for multiple agents

108
00:05:44.802 --> 00:05:47.805
all speaking the same language
with the same protocol.

109
00:05:47.972 --> 00:05:49.515
The next large piece that's on the roadmap

110
00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:53.519
for the model Context Protocol,
is the idea around a unified registry.

111
00:05:53.644 --> 00:05:56.439
The purpose of this is to standardize
the way

112
00:05:56.439 --> 00:05:59.442
in which we think about discovering
servers themselves.

113
00:05:59.609 --> 00:06:00.568
As we've seen before,

114
00:06:00.568 --> 00:06:03.071
there's a lot of excitement
in the open source community,

115
00:06:03.071 --> 00:06:05.990
and there are many different servers
for data providers.

116
00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:10.286
Tools like Google Drive, GitHub
and so on may have dozens of MCP servers,

117
00:06:10.453 --> 00:06:14.957
but just like with packages
with NPM or PyPI, there's an opportunity

118
00:06:14.957 --> 00:06:18.461
for malicious code to exist
inside of these particular servers.

119
00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:22.799
So the registry API serves
the purpose of discovering servers,

120
00:06:22.965 --> 00:06:27.512
of centralizing where these servers live,
and also verifying that these servers

121
00:06:27.512 --> 00:06:31.015
have been trusted by the community
and by companies themselves.

122
00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:34.894
This also allows
for versioning of particular MCP servers

123
00:06:35.103 --> 00:06:38.314
to lock in dependencies,
just like you would in your application.

124
00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:42.318
What this also gets
exciting is the ability for MCP servers

125
00:06:42.693 --> 00:06:44.695
to let agents self discover them.

126
00:06:44.695 --> 00:06:45.905
You can imagine a scenario

127
00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:49.534
where a user needs to fix
a bug based on something in some logs.

128
00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:52.787
The agent
then searches through the registry API

129
00:06:52.954 --> 00:06:57.667
for the official MCP server, installs
it and queries and suggests the fix.

130
00:06:58.251 --> 00:07:02.797
In this particular use case, instead of
requiring the application to be connected

131
00:07:03.047 --> 00:07:06.968
to a variety of servers from the start,
we can start to build applications

132
00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:11.055
where MCP servers are
dynamically discovered and connected to.

133
00:07:11.514 --> 00:07:14.183
As we think about layering this on
with authentication,

134
00:07:14.183 --> 00:07:18.479
we can imagine that a user has a request
that requires a server to be discovered.

135
00:07:18.813 --> 00:07:23.025
Similar to other protocols like OAUTH
and the agent-to-agent protocol

136
00:07:23.067 --> 00:07:24.402
that Google recently announced,

137
00:07:24.402 --> 00:07:28.197
the idea of putting in a JSON file
in a well-known folder is something

138
00:07:28.197 --> 00:07:31.742
that's been done before,
except here in this MCP JSON,

139
00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:36.164
we specify the endpoint of a server
to connect to, the capabilities

140
00:07:36.164 --> 00:07:39.625
or primitives that it exposes, and then
the authentication that's required.

141
00:07:39.876 --> 00:07:42.879
So a user might ask
how we manage my store on Shopify.

142
00:07:43.004 --> 00:07:45.173
The agent or AI application

143
00:07:45.173 --> 00:07:48.926
will see if Shopify has a
well known MCP JSON file.

144
00:07:49.302 --> 00:07:51.262
And if it does, it will figure out

145
00:07:51.262 --> 00:07:54.724
what endpoint to connect to
and what authentication is required.

146
00:07:54.932 --> 00:07:56.517
Once the user authenticates,

147
00:07:56.517 --> 00:08:00.354
the agent can perform the necessary action.
Through a registry API,

148
00:08:00.605 --> 00:08:05.276
we can allow for this idea of dynamic
discovery and through layering on Oauth2,

149
00:08:05.568 --> 00:08:08.321
we can ensure that these connections
are secure.

150
00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:11.782
As you might see in the suggestions,
and in the discussions, there's a lot more

151
00:08:11.782 --> 00:08:13.201
that's coming to the protocol.

152
00:08:13.201 --> 00:08:16.537
As more and more clients
support HTTP streamable.

153
00:08:17.038 --> 00:08:20.917
The aim is to achieve a smooth transition
between staple and stateless capabilities.

154
00:08:21.459 --> 00:08:24.462
As remote MCP servers
continue to be developed,

155
00:08:24.712 --> 00:08:28.299
it's important to expand the ecosystem
to support even more and more of those.

156
00:08:28.799 --> 00:08:32.303
As you can imagine,
when multiple MCP servers are being used,

157
00:08:32.762 --> 00:08:35.681
it's very possible
for tools to have naming conflicts.

158
00:08:35.681 --> 00:08:36.766
You can imagine servers

159
00:08:36.766 --> 00:08:40.645
that might have generic names of tools
like fetch users, fetch entities,

160
00:08:40.978 --> 00:08:43.648
and the model might get confused
about what needs to be fetched.

161
00:08:43.648 --> 00:08:45.900
So it's important
to think about preventing collisions

162
00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:48.819
and creating logical groups of servers
or tools.

163
00:08:48.819 --> 00:08:52.323
We spoke a bit about sampling
or proactively requesting context.

164
00:08:52.532 --> 00:08:55.618
There's a lot of work that's being put
into the protocol and it's conversations

165
00:08:55.618 --> 00:08:59.455
we have to enable primitives
like sampling to become much more popular.

166
00:08:59.622 --> 00:09:03.084
And finally, while OAUTH2
is relatively new to the specification,

167
00:09:03.334 --> 00:09:04.627
there's still quite a bit more to think

168
00:09:04.627 --> 00:09:07.922
about with regards to authentication
and authorization at scale.

169
00:09:08.381 --> 00:09:09.882
In just a short amount of time,

170
00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:12.301
you've seen so much about the Model
Context Protocol.

171
00:09:12.301 --> 00:09:15.972
You learn conceptually
about the primitives. You've built, servers

172
00:09:15.972 --> 00:09:19.934
and clients and hosts, and you've seen
how to deploy remote MCP servers.

173
00:09:20.309 --> 00:09:22.436
There's
still so much more to be discovered.

174
00:09:22.436 --> 00:09:26.357
So I encourage all of you to take a look
at the discussions and the conversations,

175
00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:29.652
and keep building
and researching as much as you can.

176
00:09:29.652 --> 00:09:31.320
Thank you so much for joining me
in this journey,

177
00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:33.114
and I can't wait to
see what you build with MCP.

